section('10. Progression, leadership and careers',`Next-level XP thresholds are 40, 120, 240, 400, 600, 850 and 1150. XP is spent/subtracted on each level gain; total to reach L8 is 3,400. Every defeated enemy adds its own XP immediately at authoritative battle recording; level-ups are processed on normal return. Quest-specific positive xpReward overrides the base completion XP preview; peaceful one-minute trips have their own override. Legacy open-slot XP rules remain exported, but every current embark requires all three slots, so do not design around an active small-party bonus. Dead/returning/skeleton XP presentation should be audited against actual per-character increments; skeletons do not gain levels.

Class identity uses stable class/sex/portrait keys; full appearance selections survive save/load, history/replay migration and skeleton normalization. Eight keys per class/sex are default, v2:0, v2:1, v2:2, v4:4, v4:5, v4:6, v4:7. Type labels are creation-facing appearance labels, not permanent class-name suffixes. Rename changes identity text without changing class/signature.

Personality traits and their autonomous/special-choice weights have been removed. Leadership (1-5), class expertise and practical judgment remain. Leadership succession can still replace an incapacitated/dead leader.

Bonds and their formation, attack bonus and death-triggered trait awards have been removed. Migration clears bonds and per-character personality/earned-trait/badge arrays. Compatibility helpers return no bonus or award. Old historical report text may remain as history, without mechanical effects.

Careers track participations/completions/victories, kills, damage, healing, signatures, boss victories, injuries and last quest. History also stores leaderships, death rolls, wounds, bonded deaths, notable events and nickname milestones. At level 3 a one-time nickname opportunity exists. Some helper descriptions imply stronger numerical effects than their runtime consumers currently apply; separate flavor from actual behavior.

Campaign counters persist separately from the rolling history, which is capped at 50 records. Settled-trip guards are also capped at 50. Recaps must report the actual retained currency, casualties, worn/broken gear and ritual unlocks, and repeated recap/load must not reaward anything.`);
h(3,'Order/decision compatibility catalogue');code(P.EXPEDITION_ORDERS);text('These are compatibility/domain helper definitions. Current creation/embark and fixed Tactical combat should be inspected before treating all order/stance choices as currently exposed player agency.');

section('11. Inventory, all equipment and the supply economy',`Four equipment slots: Head, Body, Off-hand, Accessory. Compatibility is a per-item class allowlist. A new equip consumes shared stock, returns the old item with its condition/broken state, recomputes permitted stats and never fills missing HP. A higher Ward maximum can supply its corresponding newly added Ward capacity; distinguish that from HP healing.

Armor is a consumable defense pool. Class Armor and equipment condition persist through quest battles. Return restores class Armor and each non-broken equipped armor item's condition. The first positive-to-zero ordinary Armor crossing per member per expedition rolls a seeded 6% chance of one equipped armor item becoming Broken. The item remains owned but contributes zero Armor until repaired. Oath's bonus alone cannot trigger ordinary gear-break accounting. Repairs cost ceil(price×0.35), and broken flags move with items through stock/equip/save/load.

The supply Potion costs 6 Gold and heals 2 HP up to max, at camp only, without reviving. Medkits are removed from visible supply state and legacy report text during migration. Hidden legacy items remain compatibility definitions; they must not be mistaken for current Shop options.

Selling uses the item's fixed floor(price/2) sellValue. Only shared unequipped stock is eligible. The current model has no per-instance randomized affix/item-level system, crafting recipe loop or quest entry fee. Do not invent these when reviewing basic economy balance.`);
table(['ID','Name','Slot/type','Buy','Sell','Armor','HP','Ward','Dodge pp','Crit pp','Classes','Legacy','Description'],Object.entries(P.SHOP).map(([id,i])=>[id,i.name,i.slot||i.type||'supply',i.price,i.sellValue,i.armor||0,i.hp||0,i.ward||0,i.dodge||0,i.crit||0,(i.classes||[]).join('/'),!!i.legacy,i.description]));
h(3,'Exact normalized Shop JSON (retains any fields not shown in the table)');code(P.SHOP);

section('12. Camp economy, healing and concrete value comparisons',`Camp advances while the game process is open, including minimized/background catch-up, but grants no closed-process credit. Each eligible living, non-retired, non-away member has saved job fractions and an independent saved recovery fraction.

Every 30 seconds, Work gives 2 Gold to Rogue/Ranger/Barbarian and 1 Gold to other classes. Study gives 2 Arcana to Mage/Cleric/Knight and 1 Arcana to the others. Thus a single specialized worker/student produces 240 resource/hour; three specialized members produce 720/hour. Mixed classes have a lower maximum for a chosen currency. All current active slots travel together, so the ordinary three-person company does not simultaneously earn camp production during its expedition.

Recovery is +1 HP per 30 seconds while below max and happens alongside Idle, Work or Study. It does not remove permanent wound titles or lasting-injury stacks. This is different from older comments promising full HP after one minute or one resource per minute. Potion healing is 3 Gold/HP before cap waste; compare its convenience against automatic concurrent camp recovery rather than assuming paid healing is the only recovery sink.

Gear acquisition is the main recurring Gold investment. Death returns gear to stock, so the strongest material loss is not automatic gear deletion: identity, level investment, permanent wounds and revival costs supply the other stakes. Free Tavern replacement is an intentional recovery route, and a replacement's level/identity loss must be included in campaign-value calculations.

Greed Prayer is a fixed 1 Arcana → 1 Gold conversion in 100-unit batches. Arcana and Gold therefore have an available exchange relationship after that unlock. It does not mean they have identical subjective value, production specialties or opportunity costs.

Fortune costs 180 Arcana and needs 720 base/collected Gold to yield 180 extra coins, ignoring currency valuation and rounding. Offering costs 200, adds 10 Arcana per battle win, and breaks even on its bonus alone at 20 wins. Rebate costs 150 and grants 50, net -100 Arcana while forfeiting coins; it is no longer an Arcana faucet. All remain optional expensive choices, and their fixed effects are preserved.

No carrying capacity, food/upkeep, durability degradation per real-world day, quest admission payment or automatic offline income is present. Proposed economy polish should first measure existing faucets, sinks, active-session opportunity cost and player pacing instead of introducing a new mandatory expense system.`);
h(3,'Per-class camp rates');table(['Class','Work /30s','Study /30s','Work /hour','Study /hour','HP /30s while hurt'],P.ALL_CLASSES.map(c=>{const w=['rogue','ranger','barbarian'].includes(c)?2:1,a=['mage','cleric','knight'].includes(c)?2:1;return[c,w,a,w*120,a*120,1];}));

section('13. Injury, death, resurrection and persistent attrition',`Healthy, Wounded and Dead are separate persistent conditions; alive=false also makes conditionOf report Dead. During an expedition a survived knockout is represented in preparedTeam as health=0, alive=false and incapacitated=true, while its persistent character is Wounded/alive at 1 HP. This intentional split means the casualty is saved but cannot reappear on the road or in the next fight. Current presentation filters casualties by expedition injury/death state.

Each ordinary survived knockout adds one lasting-injury stack and a next available permanent wound title. The existing titles are Missing Eye, Bad Knee, Mangled Hand, Scarred, Nerve Damage and Badly Healed Ribs. Shared penalties are −2 percentage points Dodge per stack (floor zero) and initiative ×0.97 per stack in prepared combat. Titles do not add a second copy of those penalties. More stacks can exist than distinct titles.

Healing removes one actual permanent title and its stack; removing the final title clears remaining stacks/condition according to the existing rule. It restores no HP. Ordinary HP recovery does not remove these permanent effects. Review the title/stack relationship carefully when designing clearer injury feedback.

Human knockout fate is an exact 50/50 in current combat, including healthy characters. Legacy d20 death-save helpers and old “injured characters alone risk death” prose do not describe the current timed battle result path. Death is authoritative in the persistent roster, graveyard, active slots, teams, prepared figures and leader replacement.

Death returns equipped items to stock with condition/broken metadata, marks the record retired/dead, removes it from active slots and updates history. It does not delete the remembered character record. A vacancy blocks embark until filled; Tavern offers free replacements.

Reanimate returns the fallen record with the matching skeleton art, the same class/level and current living class maxima, preserves wounds/powers/history, and disables leveling. Potions, Cleric healing and normal camp HP recovery still work. Its later knockout is death without a survival coin; reanimationExhausted is permanently set and also inferred for old dead-skeleton saves. Reload/Last Rites does not clear this flag. Last Rites is a separate return-at-1-HP-with-injury ritual, so decide explicitly whether its current ability to return a terminal skeleton is intended rather than assuming the Reanimate prohibition forbids every other rite.

Mercy is the exception during its next-quest scope: after an actual would-die outcome it consumes one shared charge, keeps that member active at 1 HP, and applies a serious permanent wound/stack using the same real penalties. An ordinary survived knockout does not consume Mercy. It must not become an unconditional heal, a per-member shield or a repeat resurrection.`);
h(3,'Wound definitions');code(P.WOUND_DEFINITIONS);

h(2,'14. All fourteen rituals: costs, unlocks, scopes and edge cases');
table(['Ritual / ID','Arcana/use','Unlock quest','Level','Effect'],Object.entries(P.RITUALS).map(([id,r])=>[r.name+' / '+id,r.castCost,r.questName+' / '+r.questId,r.level,r.description]));
text('Queued casts cannot duplicate-stack the same pending effect and cannot be activated while away. Different effects can coexist. Living targets must be active camp members; Healing requires a real wound. Fallen targets require a free active slot or the existing explicit replacement confirmation. Failed/locked/invalid/duplicate casts charge nothing.');
text('Pending effects transfer atomically to trip.ritualEffects on departure. Warding and Battle Hymn carry back if no relevant hit/battle consumed them. Next-quest effects expire with that expedition even if it is peaceful. Familiar intercepts before Warding when both are present. Fortune has no Ward requirement or penalty; it only multiplies settled coins by 1.25, rounded down.');
text('Rebate suppresses completion and gathered coins and adds 50 Arcana on return, including recall. Offering adds 10 per recorded victory, not per enemy kill or wave. Battle victories are idempotently counted at result recording. One-off healing/exchange/revival effects apply immediately at camp. Quest-only unlocks require a completed victory, not an abandoned/automatic-retreat victory or a debug currency payment. Legacy purchased unlock flags are replaced by quest-earned state on migration; ritualUnlock now refuses purchase and points to the required quest.');
text('Quickening uses the existing seeded d20 with 16–20 as success, independently per adventurer per battle of the next quest. It does not unlock a low-level special or double unrelated one-off refinements/capstones. First Strike is the first attack attempt, so a dodge spends it. Oath is a consumable temporary Armor point on one chosen member, with no dedicated target/protection effect; normal enemy defense-aware scoring still sees the unit’s total defense.');
text('Ritual information appears in the altar, encounter support/mercy captions and report unlock lines. Shared ritual charges are not currently represented as three individual member Ward counters. Review observability of pending/consumed/expired effects and failed-cast messages as polish opportunities.');

